j3c 0.1.0 — J3 compiler

usage:
    j3c <command> [file.j3 | project]

commands:
    new [name]       write j3.toml, src/main.j3, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md (hello)
    new [name] --app SDL window (links SDL2)
    new [name] --template fps  first-person 3D game with a scene (SDL2 + GL)
    new [name] --template ui   .j3ui components window (SDL2 + ttf + GL; `doc j3ui`)
    new [name] --lib a package other projects import (`doc packages`)
    add NAME --path DIR | --git URL [--tag T]  add a dependency to j3.toml and fetch it
    fetch            download git dependencies, write j3.lock
    update [name]    move git dependencies to their newest commit, rewrite j3.lock
    pkg [NAME [CMD args...]]  list package tools, or build and run one (`doc packages`)
    editor [--new] [project]  open the 3D scene editor (not in this build yet: use -e scene / -e render)
    -e scene <op> ... list/get/add/set/delete/check scene objects by name (`-e scene --help`)
    -e render [opts] draw a scene to a PNG without a window (`-e render --help`)
    -e gen [--force] run assets/gen/*.j3 mesh generators that changed
    -e mcp [--project D] MCP server for agents (not in this build yet: use -e scene / -e render)
    -e mcp --tools   every MCP tool's arguments and the CLI command doing the same
    -e android run [dir] build the APK, install it on an adb device, start it, print its log (`-e android --help`)
    -e web run DIR   serve a built web folder and run it (headless Chrome with --headless) (`-e web --help`)
    -e script-props <f.j3s>  a script's props as JSON (what the inspector edits)
                     (-e is short for --editor)
    guide            how to start a project
    doctor           check this machine: clang, linker, SDL2 libs, GL, fonts, cache (exit 1 = not ready)
    check [path]     typecheck; no emit or link
    check --json     one JSON object on stdout
    explain E0042    what an `error[E0042]:` code means and how to fix it
    doc [module]     tasks and stdlib modules, or one module's public items (`doc --help`)
    doc search <w>   public names containing a word, in any module
    doc recipes      complete checked programs: 2D app, 3D scene, canvas, script, ...
    doc lang         language
    doc j3ui         .j3ui components (j3ui-immediate: @j3ui functions)
    doc css          CSS subset for .j3ui
    doc testing      headless run, screenshots, goldens, imgdiff
    golden <dir> [names] [--bless]  headless goldens of a project: states in <dir>/golden.toml (`doc testing`)
    -e imgdiff a.png b.png [--tol N] [--max N] [--out d.png]  pixel diff for goldens (exit 1 = differs)
    build [path]     emit, compile, write `target/<name>`
    run [path]       emit, compile, execute
    run --frames N [--screenshot F.png] [--headless]  stop an App after N frames;
                     F.png is relative to where you run j3c, not the project
    run --file f.j3  run that one file even inside a project
    run tool.j3s [args]  run that script's `pub fn main` once; args are `j3.sys.arg`
    fmt [path]       pretty-print `.j3` and `.j3s` (same lexer; keeps comments)
    fmt --write      rewrite files
    fmt --check      exit 1 if formatting would change a file
    lsp              language server (not in this build yet: `check --json` for diagnostics)
    dump-ast <file|dir>  print the parsed AST as S-expressions (the parse oracle)
    dump-ir [path]   parse + typecheck; print IR
    dump-ir samples  print hand-built place-IR samples
    dump-llvm [path] print LLVM IR from the program
    dump-glsl [path] [--es]  print the GLSL of each `@shader(vs, fs)` (`j3c doc shaders`)
    dump-ui <file.j3ui|dir> print the J3 generated from .j3ui components
    dump-bc <file.j3s> print script bytecode
    dump-syntax [--ui] [f]  print TextMate grammar (lexer/parser names; --ui for .j3ui)
    --fast           with run/build: strip bounds-check Abort; no `-g`
    --emit llvm|bc   backend (default llvm; bc is `.j3s` script bytecode; override [emit] backend)
    --opt N          0/1/2/3/s/z (override [emit] opt)
    --release        with run/build: smallest binary (opt s, no `-g`, stripped; keeps checks); build also writes [dist] build_outputs (zip, deb, or a package output: `j3c doc ship`); `j3.build.RELEASE`/`DEV` say which
    size [path]      what is in `target/<name>`: sections, code per module, biggest functions
    run samples      emit, compile, execute place tests
    help manifest    print j3.toml keys
    manifest --json [path]  the project's j3.toml as JSON (every table), for scripts and package tools
    version          print version
    version --verbose  docs id, stdlib source, binary path: spot a stale j3c
    help             print this help

A path may be a `.j3` file, a `j3.toml`, or a directory with `j3.toml`.
With no path, run, build, and check use ./j3.toml and stop if it is missing.
dump-ir and dump-llvm still fall back to the built-in hello.
emit: LLVM text compiled by clang. Options live in [emit] of j3.toml.
run/build of a project executes with cwd = package root (`J3_ROOT`).
J3C_TIMINGS=1 prints how long each build phase took to stderr (`j3c help manifest`).
`j3c guide` is the short start. `j3c doc` lists the standard library.
--- stderr
--- exit 0
